  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Un centro comunitario en Tarapoto, como modelo para el fortalecimiento del rescate de fauna silvestre en la Amazonia Peruana</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Peru is a source and conduit for wildlife trafficking of many species listed as threatened with extinction or vulnerable to future extinction. From 2009 to 2012, Perus wildlife agency (SERFOR) registered 13,033 confiscated wildlife (approximately 3,258 per year). Only a small number of wildlife confiscated by the authorities is rehabilitated and returned to their natural habitats and the available rescue centers operate on a not-for-profit basis with little to no support from the local governments. This project will contribute to the implementation of Perus National Strategy to Combat Wildlife Trafficking by working on the front-lines in a region where there are important transit and interdiction points for wildlife trafficking, and the regional environmental authority has made progress in their wildlife control efforts with the support of the Urku Center due to its community-based environmental activities with indigenous communities and its work with rescuing, rehabilitating and reintroducing confiscated wildlife. Specific activities include updating and implementing the Centers management plan and wildlife protocols; training for staff on improved wildlife rehabilitation and reintroduction practices; training for wildlife authorities on wildlife handling procedures; and a public awareness campaign on the impacts of wildlife trafficking in the region.</narrative>
  </description>
